<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Karana</h1><p class="st-meta">1780 BCE – 1761 BCE (approx.) · capital Karana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Mesopotamian kingdom of the Mari age, centred on Tell al-Rimah, whose queen Iltani left a remarkable archive of letters.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Karana lay south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar-mountains">Jebel Sinjar</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-al-rimah">Tell al-Rimah</a>, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-oates">David Oates</a> in the 1960s, is usually identified as Karana or its twin city Qattara. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> around 1776 BCE, Karana became an independent kingdom under Asqur-Addu, son of its earlier king Samu-Addu, and later under Aqba-hammu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Karana's kings competed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-andarig">Andarig</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kurda">Kurda</a> and swore alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> and other powers. The temple and palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-al-rimah">Tell al-Rimah</a> contained tablets of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltani">Iltani</a>, wife of King Aqba-hammu, who managed textile production, grain and personnel and corresponded with her family, a rare view of a royal woman's administration. The kingdom rested on dry-farmed barley and sheep.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Aqba-hammu became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi of Babylon</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> in 1761 BCE, and Karana lost its independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-al-rimah">Tell al-Rimah</a> continued to be occupied in later periods.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1780 BCE – 1761 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:24.28%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1780 BCE – 1761 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Asqur-Addu, Aqba-hammu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karana, Qattara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1770&amp;focus=kingdom-of-karana" aria-label="Kingdom of Karana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5795 960 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Karana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6143 1252h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6115 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6115 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-1770&amp;focus=kingdom-of-karana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

